<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jama Masjid, Bijapur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jami Masjid of Bijapur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jama Masjid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> is the congregational mosque of the Adil Shahi capital, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i">Ali Adil Shah I</a> in the later sixteenth century. Its large prayer hall under a broad central dome made it one of the biggest mosques in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. The building was never fully completed by its founders; the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> added an entrance gateway after his conquest of Bijapur in 1686.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jama-masjid-bijapur.jpg" alt="Jama Masjid, Bijapur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rahiaql · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JUMA_MASJID_BIJAPUR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i">Ali Adil Shah I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a> began the mosque in 1576, paying for it with money looted at the Battle of Talikota, in which an alliance of the Deccan Sultanates had defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. Most of the building was standing by 1686, but it never reached completion. It nonetheless became the principal mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, taking the place of an older and smaller congregational mosque built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-i">Ibrahim Adil Shah I</a>. Later rulers added to it: the murals around the central mihrab were probably made under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-adil-shah">Muhammad Adil Shah</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> altered the flooring of the prayer hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The rectangular complex covers 5,040 square metres within perimeter walls and is entered by its eastern gate. A square courtyard 50 metres on each side holds fountains and an ablution reservoir. The prayer hall on the west, 70 by 36 metres, has a facade of seven arches, only the central one decorated, and a hemispherical dome with a crescent-moon finial and a balustrade at its base. Inside, piers divide it into five bays parallel to the qibla wall, and its floor is marked into 2250 rectangles like prayer mats, a later addition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>.</p><p>The design is plain, and ArchNet explains this by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shiism</a> of Ali Adil Shah, as followers of that branch often preferred little decoration in places of worship. The mihrab breaks the austerity with gilded murals in blue, black and gold dominated by Quranic writing; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-adil-shah">Muhammad Adil Shah</a>, unlike Ali, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>. Corner buttresses on the eastern facade show that two minarets were planned, and the gallery parapets lack their merlons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The mosque is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, holding 4,000 worshippers, and is counted among the finest works of Adil Shahi architecture. Bianca Alfieri traces elements of earlier Bahmanid building in it, and Richard Eaton sees it as showing Iranian influence more than local traditions. Michell and Zebrowski rank its mihrab among the finest in the Islamic world, comparing it to that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</a>. In 2014 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> placed it on its tentative list as part of the Monuments and Forts of the Deccan Sultanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jama Mosque, Bijapur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Jami Masjid</b>, the Asar Mahal and the city walls complete one of the richest groups of Islamic monuments in India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
